.TH MACHO\-SYM 1 "October 2, 2026" "MachO-Tools 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
macho\-sym \- search a Mach\-O symbol table
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B macho\-sym
.I pattern
.RI [ binary | bundle ]
.RI [ max ]
.RB [ \-\-regex ]
.RB [ \-\-case\-sensitive ]
.RB [ \-\-all\-imp ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-dedupe ]
.RB [ \-\-arch= name ]
.RB [ \-b | \-\-binary
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Searches the symbol table of one slice of a Mach\-O file and prints the
matching symbols with their virtual addresses.
.PP
The pattern is matched as a plain substring by default, or as a regular
expression with
.BR \-\-regex .
Substring is the default because a symbol name is usually known without its
exact spelling, and a pattern that fails to compile as a regex is a worse
failure than one that matches more than intended.
.PP
Imported symbols are excluded unless
.B \-\-all\-imp
is given. An imported name carries no address and no implementation, so it does
not answer "where is this implemented"; with
.B \-\-all\-imp
such names are included and marked
.B (import)
rather than printed as
.BR 0x0 ,
because a bare zero reads as "defined at address zero".
.PP
One row is printed per distinct name unless
.B \-\-no\-dedupe
is given. A symbol table routinely carries one name at several addresses
(aliases, thunks, per\-architecture copies), and a list where
.B memcpy
appears nine times is harder to read than one where it appears once.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-arch= name
Prefer one architecture by name:
.BR x86_64 ,
.BR arm64 ,
.BR arm64e ,
.BR arm64_32 ,
.BR ppc ,
.BR ppc64 ,
.BR arm
or
.BR i386 .
This is a preference, not a requirement: an architecture that is absent falls
back to the richest slice rather than failing.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-binary " " \fIpath\fR
The binary to read, for use when every positional argument is part of the
query.
.TP
.BR \-\-case\-sensitive
Match case exactly. Without it, matching is case\-insensitive.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write one JSON object to standard output and send all prose to standard error,
so that output can be piped into a parser without reading diagnostics as data.
Addresses are rendered as
.B "0x..."
strings: a 64\-bit virtual address does not survive a JSON number.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-dedupe
Print one row per symbol table entry rather than one per distinct name.
.TP
.I max
Cap on the number of rows, when deduplicating. The default is 4000.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MACHO_BINARY
The binary to read when none is given on the command line.
.TP
.B MACHO_APP
An application bundle to read, tried after
.BR MACHO_BINARY .
.TP
.B MACHO_CONFIG
Path to a replacement
.IR config.json .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one match.
.TP
.B 1
The tool ran and found nothing. This is deliberately distinct from failure: a
caller that treats "no matches" as an error treats a working tool as broken.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 3
The file could not be read, or the chosen slice has no symbol table.
.SH EXAMPLES
Find a symbol by substring:
.PP
.RS
.nf
macho\-sym runtime.main /usr/local/go/bin/go
.fi
.RE
.PP
Search with a regular expression:
.PP
.RS
.nf
macho\-sym \-\-regex 'runtime\..*main' /usr/local/go/bin/go
.fi
.RE
.PP
Count matches without reading the human\-readable report:
.PP
.RS
.nf
macho\-sym \-\-json compress /usr/bin/ssh | jq '.count'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Include imported names, one row per entry:
.PP
.RS
.nf
macho\-sym \-\-all\-imp \-\-no\-dedupe memcpy /usr/bin/ssh
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
This tool was previously two, called
.B macho\-symgrep
(regex, defined symbols only, one row per entry) and
.B macho\-symfind
(substring, imports included, deduplicated). Both were removed in 0.1.0 rather
than kept as aliases. The equivalent invocations are
.B macho\-sym \-\-regex
and
.BR "macho\-sym \-\-all\-imp" .
.PP
A stripped binary has no symbol names to search. This tool then reports no
matches rather than guessing;
.BR macho\-symlookup (1)
and
.BR macho\-findcall (1)
read addresses and bytes instead and still work.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR macho\-describe (1),
.BR macho\-symlookup (1),
.BR macho\-findcall (1),
.BR macho\-findliteral (1),
.BR macho\-mapliteral (1),
.BR macho\-a2o (1),
.BR macho\-o2a (1),
.BR otool (1),
.BR nm (1),
.BR lipo (1)